    "originalStyle": "This track is a high-energy  Rockabilly  piece that leans into classic 1950s rock-and-roll with a modern, slightly lo-fi edge.\n It sits at  ≈143 BPM  in  4/4  time and is rooted in  F major .\n\n Instrumentation & production:  The arrangement is built around a twangy electric guitar that delivers bright, single-note riffs and occasional double-stop bends, a walking upright bass that drives the groove with a percussive, “boom-chick” feel, and a tight drum kit (snare, kick, hi-hat) that keeps a steady shuffle rhythm. The mix is deliberately raw: minimal processing, modest compression, and a modest amount of tape-style saturation give the recording a vintage, live-room ambience. The stereo field is modestly wide, with the guitar panned slightly left, bass centered, and drums spread for a natural stage feel.\n\n Vocals:  A male lead sings in a mid-range, slightly nasal timbre that is characteristic of early rock-and-roll crooners. The delivery is clean, melodic, and energetic, with virtually no effects beyond a touch of room reverb, preserving an authentic, unpolished sound.\n\n Lyrical themes:  The lyrics revolve around a character named Lisa, portraying her as carefree and independent. The chorus repeatedly chants the hook  “Lisa says, Lisa says, Lisa says, Lisa says,”  reinforcing the song's playful, almost tongue-in-cheek narrative. Verses add details about her wandering spirit (“Lisa says that she's on the run…”) and a hint of melancholy (“she knows her heart will break”), but the overall tone remains light-hearted.\n\n Song structure & dynamics:   \n-  Intro:  Brief guitar riff establishing the main groove.  \n-  Verse:  Walking bass and shuffle drums underpin the vocal narrative.  \n-  Chorus:  The repetitive “Lisa says” hook arrives, with the full band accentuating the rhythm and a slight lift in volume.  \n-  Instrumental break:  A short guitar solo over the chord progression adds excitement before returning to the verse.  \n-  Bridge/Breakdown:  Minimalist texture, often dropping to just bass and drums, creating a brief dynamic dip.  \n-  Final chorus & outro:  The hook repeats with added backing vocals and a final guitar flourish, ending on a crisp F major chord.\n\n Harmonic analysis:  The progression cycles through classic I-IV-V movements (F-Bb-C) with occasional color tones such as Bb6, C7, and a brief Gm7♭5/F passing chord, giving the track a blues-inflected flavor while staying firmly in the major key. These changes reinforce the upbeat, dance-able mood typical of rockabilly.\n\n Overall mood & context:  The song exudes a buoyant, nostalgic vibe that feels like a live club performance from the 1950s, yet its slightly gritty production places it in a contemporary indie-rockabilly revival scene. It captures the carefree spirit of early rock-and-roll while delivering a fresh, energetic listening experience.",
